1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:06,519
September twenty fifteen. The new iPhone costs six hundred and forty nine dollars, and you've been saving for months.

2
00:00:06,839 --> 00:00:12,180
What if, instead, you'd spent that money on a strange internet coin called Bitcoin?

3
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:19,361
Today, that phone would be a museum piece. And the coins would be worth over one hundred and sixty six thousand dollars.

4
00:00:19,681 --> 00:00:25,196
Sounds like the easiest decision in history. But this story has a twist that almost nobody talks about.

5
00:00:25,516 --> 00:00:30,230
To get that money, you would have had to survive three crashes, each one of them brutal.

6
00:00:31,030 --> 00:00:37,357
Let's go back. You're standing in line at the phone store. Max is next to you, holding his phone like a newborn.

7
00:00:37,677 --> 00:00:40,975
Max: Bro, the camera on this thing. Life changing.

8
00:00:41,295 --> 00:00:49,143
But you've been reading strange forums at three in the morning. Internet money. No banks. Only twenty one million coins will ever exist.

9
00:00:49,463 --> 00:00:51,100
And here the timeline splits.

10
00:00:51,900 --> 00:00:58,146
Quick explanation, in case you skipped those forums.

11
00:00:58,466 --> 00:01:07,452
Bitcoin is digital money that isn't run by a bank or a government. Transactions are recorded on a public ledger that thousands of computers keep in sync.

12
00:01:07,772 --> 00:01:12,614
Its rules say there will never be more than twenty one million coins. That scarcity is a big part of why people buy it.

13
00:01:12,934 --> 00:01:16,405
Its price isn't set by anyone. It's simply whatever the next buyer is willing to pay. And that can change very, very fast.

14
00:01:17,205 --> 00:01:20,224
In timeline A, you buy the phone. It's beautiful. You drop it on the first day. It survives. You love it.

15
00:01:20,544 --> 00:01:29,669
In timeline B, you open an exchange account and buy six hundred and forty nine dollars of Bitcoin, at about two hundred and thirty six dollars per coin.

16
00:01:29,989 --> 00:01:35,631
You now own two point seven five bitcoins. And you still have your old cracked phone.

17
00:01:35,951 --> 00:01:38,877
Max thinks you've been scammed. Honestly, so does your bank.

18
00:01:39,197 --> 00:01:45,234
Your bank even calls to double check the payment. Yes, you say. Yes, I really meant to buy internet money.

19
00:01:46,034 --> 00:01:47,741
For two years, timeline B looks like a genius.

20
00:01:48,061 --> 00:01:54,893
In December twenty seventeen, Bitcoin hits about nineteen thousand dollars. Your six hundred and forty nine dollars are now worth over fifty three thousand.

21
00:01:55,213 --> 00:01:59,346
Taxi drivers are talking about crypto. Your aunt asks you how to buy some. Max is furious.

22
00:01:59,666 --> 00:02:05,518
Timeline A's phone is already slowing down.

23
00:02:06,318 --> 00:02:10,695
Then, crash number one.

24
00:02:11,015 --> 00:02:16,808
By December twenty eighteen, Bitcoin is around three thousand two hundred dollars. Down about eighty four percent from the top.

25
00:02:17,128 --> 00:02:22,085
Your fifty three thousand dollars are now about eight thousand seven hundred.

26
00:02:22,405 --> 00:02:26,806
You lost forty four thousand dollars of paper money in one year. People online are calling Bitcoin dead.

27
00:02:27,126 --> 00:02:31,096
Every part of you wants to sell what's left and buy a very nice phone.

28
00:02:31,896 --> 00:02:33,278
Meanwhile, Max has had enough of hearing about it. In December twenty seventeen, right at the top, he finally buys six hundred and forty nine dollars of Bitcoin too.

29
00:02:33,598 --> 00:02:41,713
At about nineteen thousand dollars a coin, he gets a tiny slice. Three hundredths of a bitcoin.

30
00:02:42,033 --> 00:02:49,104
One year later, his six hundred and forty nine dollars are worth about one hundred and seven dollars.

31
00:02:49,424 --> 00:02:56,076
Max sells, swears at the internet, and goes back to phones.

32
00:02:56,396 --> 00:03:01,133
And the painful part? If Max had simply held that tiny slice until November twenty twenty six, it would be worth about two thousand and thirty dollars. Triple his money.

33
00:03:01,453 --> 00:03:06,085
Same coin. Same amount of money. The only difference was the date, and the nerves.

34
00:03:06,885 --> 00:03:08,894
You don't sell. Mostly because you forgot your password for a while. That counts.

35
00:03:09,214 --> 00:03:14,949
Then twenty twenty one happens. In November, Bitcoin goes near sixty seven thousand dollars.

36
00:03:15,269 --> 00:03:20,389
Your six hundred and forty nine dollars are now worth about one hundred and eighty four thousand.

37
00:03:20,709 --> 00:03:24,436
You could buy two hundred and eighty iPhones. You don't. You just stare at the screen.

38
00:03:25,236 --> 00:03:29,601
Crash number two.

39
00:03:29,921 --> 00:03:36,597
In twenty twenty two, one of the biggest crypto exchanges in the world, FTX, collapses. Customers can't get their money out.

40
00:03:36,917 --> 00:03:42,520
By November, Bitcoin is under sixteen thousand. Your one hundred and eighty four thousand became forty three thousand.

41
00:03:42,840 --> 00:03:51,025
If your coins had been sitting on the wrong exchange, they might have been stuck or gone, no matter what the price did.

42
00:03:51,825 --> 00:03:54,983
And then, the biggest wave yet.

43
00:03:55,303 --> 00:04:02,200
On October fifth, twenty twenty five, Bitcoin hits about one hundred and twenty four thousand dollars.

44
00:04:02,520 --> 00:04:08,847
Your six hundred and forty nine dollars are now worth three hundred and forty three thousand dollars.

45
00:04:09,167 --> 00:04:13,834
That's a house deposit in many cities. From skipping one phone.

46
00:04:14,634 --> 00:04:16,782
And crash number three. Over the following year, Bitcoin falls again.

47
00:04:17,102 --> 00:04:24,405
On November twenty fifth, twenty twenty six, it's around sixty thousand six hundred dollars. About half of the peak.

48
00:04:24,725 --> 00:04:32,295
Your three hundred and forty three thousand dollars are now about one hundred and sixty six thousand.

49
00:04:32,615 --> 00:04:38,930
Still an incredible result. But imagine watching one hundred and seventy six thousand dollars disappear in a year, and doing nothing.

50
00:04:39,730 --> 00:04:52,141
Why is holding so hard? Because losses hurt more than gains feel good.

51
00:04:52,461 --> 00:04:58,893
Psychologists call it loss aversion. Watching forty four thousand dollars vanish feels far worse than gaining the same amount feels nice.

52
00:04:59,213 --> 00:05:03,323
So most people sell exactly when it hurts the most. Which is usually near the bottom.

53
00:05:03,643 --> 00:05:10,516
Timeline B didn't win because it was smart. It won because it sat still through things that made almost everyone else run.

54
00:05:11,316 --> 00:05:15,333
Meanwhile, timeline A. The iPhone six s.

55
00:05:15,653 --> 00:05:18,927
It gave you photos, maps, music, and about four years of a good life. Then a slow battery, then a drawer.

56
00:05:19,247 --> 00:05:23,961
Today, it's worth very little. But to be fair, a phone is not an investment. You use it, and that's the point.

57
00:05:24,281 --> 00:05:29,738
The question isn't phone versus coin. It's what you do with money you don't need for daily life.

58
00:05:30,058 --> 00:05:37,117
If the money is for living, spend it on living. If it's spare money for the long term, that's when the timelines really start to split.

59
00:05:37,437 --> 00:05:45,215
And for most people, the boring long term choice, like a broad index fund, does a lot of the heavy lifting without needing nerves of steel.

60
00:05:46,015 --> 00:05:51,867
Now the part most crypto videos skip.

61
00:05:52,187 --> 00:05:57,551
Very few people actually held from twenty fifteen to today. Most sold at the first big gain, or the first big crash.

62
00:05:57,871 --> 00:06:00,796
Plenty of other coins from those years lost almost everything. Exchanges failed. People lost passwords and keys forever.

63
00:06:01,116 --> 00:06:07,409
When you hear a Bitcoin success story, you're hearing from the timeline that survived. You don't hear from the ones that didn't.

64
00:06:07,729 --> 00:06:12,663
That's called survivorship bias. And it makes every risky bet look easier than it was.

65
00:06:13,463 --> 00:06:18,432
For comparison, the same six hundred and forty nine dollars in an S and P five hundred index fund in September twenty fifteen would be about two thousand five hundred and sixty dollars, counting price only.

66
00:06:18,752 --> 00:06:24,209
Far less exciting. Also far less terrifying. Its worst drops in these years were nowhere near eighty four percent.

67
00:06:24,529 --> 00:06:26,340
Bitcoin's result is real. So is the risk that came with it.

68
00:06:27,140 --> 00:06:31,227
So, should you buy Bitcoin today because of this story? This video can't answer that, and nobody honestly can.

69
00:06:31,547 --> 00:06:35,146
The past ten years happened once. The next ten could look completely different, up or down.

70
00:06:35,466 --> 00:06:40,121
What we can learn is about behaviour, not about the coin.

71
00:06:40,441 --> 00:06:45,829
Big rewards almost always come with big drops. If you can't handle the drop, you won't be there for the reward.

72
00:06:46,149 --> 00:06:50,386
And money you need in the next few years has no business riding a rollercoaster.

73
00:06:51,186 --> 00:06:56,759
So if you're thinking about crypto, here's what the careful people usually say.

74
00:06:57,079 --> 00:07:02,559
Only put in money you could lose completely and still sleep at night.

75
00:07:02,879 --> 00:07:05,897
Keep it a small slice, not your emergency fund, and never borrowed money.

76
00:07:06,217 --> 00:07:11,558
Learn how storage, scams and fees work before you buy anything. Scammers love beginners.

77
00:07:12,358 --> 00:07:17,176
And the honest part. This is not financial advice, and definitely not a recommendation to buy Bitcoin.

78
00:07:17,496 --> 00:07:22,686
Crypto prices are extremely volatile, and past returns tell you nothing certain about the future.

79
00:07:23,006 --> 00:07:24,689
Rules and taxes differ by country. Check yours.

80
00:07:25,489 --> 00:07:30,598
One phone. Two point seven five coins. Three crashes. And a lot of nerves.

81
00:07:30,918 --> 00:07:36,316
Be honest in the comments. Would you have held through all three crashes, or sold in twenty eighteen?

82
00:07:36,636 --> 00:07:38,482
And I'll see you in the other timeline.

